00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:25.700
By the end of this course, you'll be able to confidently build, train, and deploy machine learning models in the real world.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.200
The topic for today is linear regression with Scikit-Learn.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.600
This is the first topic we are covering in machine learning with Python.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:41.900
And in fact, one of the things we'll try and do today is to apply linear regression to a real world problem.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:47.800
And we'll try to predict medical expenses using this technique.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.800
So here's an overview of what we are going to cover today.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.400
Just a quick outline.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.800
So first we start by stating a typical problem statement for machine learning.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.800
Then we will download and explore a dataset.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.400
Then we will perform linear regression with one variable using Scikit-Learn.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.400
Then we'll perform linear regression with multiple variables.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.800
And don't worry if these terms don't make sense right now.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.700
They will by the end of this session.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:23.600
Then we'll use categorical features for machine learning.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.800
Learn how to apply that in general, not just for linear regression.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.600
We will also talk about regression coefficients and feature importance.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:38.400
How do you interpret the outputs of a model that you create?

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:45.000
And then we will apply linear regression to some other datasets.

