00:41:38.400 --> 00:41:41.400
So here's how the correlation coefficient is interpreted.

00:41:41.400 --> 00:41:42.400
It indicates two things.

00:41:42.400 --> 00:41:46.400
It indicates strength and it indicates the direction.

00:41:46.400 --> 00:41:51.400
So the greater the absolute value of the correlation coefficient, the stronger is the relationship.

00:41:51.400 --> 00:42:02.400
Which means that if you have an extreme value like minus one or one and one is pretty much the highest value that you can get, you cannot get something higher than one or lower than minus one.

00:42:02.400 --> 00:42:12.400
That indicates a perfectly linear relationship where a change in one variable is accompanied by a perfect consistent change in the other variable.

00:42:12.400 --> 00:42:16.400
Which means essentially that these points lie on a line and there's a geometric interpretation here.

00:42:16.400 --> 00:42:24.400
So if you have X and Y, let's say age and charges, and you saw this kind of a graph, that would mean perfect correlation, correlation of one.

00:42:24.400 --> 00:42:26.400
This would be positive correlation.

00:42:26.400 --> 00:42:36.400
And if you had a chart like this, where it was a line, but one increase in one led to decrease in the other, that would be a perfect negative correlation close to minus one.

00:42:36.400 --> 00:42:38.400
But you generally have something in between.

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:51.400
You have strong positive correlation where these points are distributed around the line or a weak positive correlation where there is a weak trend of some kind or no correlation at all where points are all over the place.

00:42:51.400 --> 00:42:55.400
Or you can have a weak negative or strong negative correlation, right?

00:42:55.400 --> 00:43:00.400
So that's the direction, the sign of the correlation coefficients represents the direction of the relationship.

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:02.400
If it's positive, then you have an increasing relationship.

00:43:02.400 --> 00:43:05.400
If it's negative, then you have a decreasing relationship.

00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:11.400
If the correlation coefficient is very close to zero, then you have practically no correlation.

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:12.400
Okay.

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:16.400
And the correlation coefficient has a formula and you can check out this formula.

00:43:16.400 --> 00:43:27.400
This is simply the sum of XI minus the mean of X, YI minus the mean of Y added up divided by a square root of some other quantity.

00:43:27.400 --> 00:43:31.400
I'll let you figure out what this formula means because it's not very important.

00:43:31.400 --> 00:43:40.400
It's simply a question of carefully looking at each of the terms and listing out what it means, but this is the more important interpretation that you should have.

00:43:40.400 --> 00:43:43.400
When you see correlations, you see plus one.

00:43:43.400 --> 00:43:44.400
This is what you see.

00:43:44.400 --> 00:43:47.400
You see something, let's say greater than 0.5, greater than 0.7.

00:43:47.400 --> 00:43:49.400
This is what you should imagine less than 0.5.

00:43:49.400 --> 00:43:53.400
This is what you should imagine close to zero is this and less than zero.

00:43:53.400 --> 00:43:56.400
These are the pictures you should have in mind.

00:43:56.400 --> 00:43:57.400
Okay.

