01:04:24.700 --> 01:04:29.700
Now we want to compare our models predictions with the actual targets.

01:04:29.700 --> 01:04:31.700
And this is the method that we can follow.

01:04:31.700 --> 01:04:39.700
We calculate the difference between the targets, which is the actual charges from the dataset and the predictions from our model.

01:04:39.700 --> 01:04:43.700
And this difference is called the residual.

01:04:43.700 --> 01:04:45.700
So for each target, you have a bunch of targets here.

01:04:45.700 --> 01:04:48.700
The targets are simply the actual charges.

01:04:48.700 --> 01:04:54.700
So we have non-smoker DF not charges.

01:04:54.700 --> 01:04:56.700
These are the targets.

01:04:56.700 --> 01:04:59.700
And then you have the predictions, which is the estimated charges.

01:04:59.700 --> 01:05:02.700
This is what our model has predicted for the input data.

01:05:02.700 --> 01:05:10.700
So predictions is estimated charges.

01:05:10.700 --> 01:05:11.700
Okay.

01:05:11.700 --> 01:05:14.700
And this is what a particular value of our model is.

01:05:14.700 --> 01:05:21.700
So what we do is we subtract each prediction from the corresponding target.

01:05:21.700 --> 01:05:25.700
So this is going to tell us how far away our prediction is from a target.

01:05:25.700 --> 01:05:28.700
But the problem is some predictions may be lower than the target.

01:05:28.700 --> 01:05:30.700
Some predictions may be higher than the target.

01:05:30.700 --> 01:05:36.700
So to remove negative values, we square all the elements in the difference vector.

01:05:36.700 --> 01:05:38.700
Let me remove this matrix term here.

01:05:38.700 --> 01:05:44.700
Yeah. So we square all the elements to remove negative values.

01:05:44.700 --> 01:05:48.700
So you subtract 1000 minus one, seven, two, five, that's seven, 25.

01:05:48.700 --> 01:05:49.700
And then you square it.

01:05:49.700 --> 01:05:53.700
So 25 squared, then you do 1500 minus four, four, four, nine.

01:05:53.700 --> 01:05:56.700
That's again, some negative value, a square it at some points.

01:05:56.700 --> 01:05:57.700
You may have positive values.

01:05:57.700 --> 01:06:00.700
Maybe you have 2,600 here and 1,600 here.

01:06:00.700 --> 01:06:02.700
So you get a positive value.

01:06:02.700 --> 01:06:03.700
Square it.

01:06:03.700 --> 01:06:08.700
So we take the residuals.

01:06:08.700 --> 01:06:11.700
Each difference is called a residual and we square all the residuals.

01:06:11.700 --> 01:06:15.700
Then we calculate the average of all those elements in the result, right?

01:06:15.700 --> 01:06:17.700
So we have all of these residuals.

01:06:17.700 --> 01:06:21.700
We simply add up all the squares of residuals and calculate the average.

01:06:21.700 --> 01:06:27.700
So now we have this minus the actual target squared plus this minus the actual target squared

01:06:27.700 --> 01:06:30.700
plus this minus the actual target squared divided by the total target.

01:06:30.700 --> 01:06:33.700
And finally, we take the square root of the result.

01:06:33.700 --> 01:06:34.700
Okay.

01:06:34.700 --> 01:06:37.700
Seems like a complicated process, but it's actually fairly straightforward.

01:06:37.700 --> 01:06:41.700
What it's giving us is called the root mean squared error.

01:06:41.700 --> 01:06:42.700
So let's break that down.

01:06:42.700 --> 01:06:46.700
Error is simply the difference between the targets and the predictions.

01:06:46.700 --> 01:06:47.700
So that is the error.

01:06:47.700 --> 01:06:51.700
But of course, if you just add up errors, positives and negatives may cancel out.

01:06:51.700 --> 01:06:53.700
So that's why we square all the elements in the result.

01:06:53.700 --> 01:06:56.700
And then we take the square root of the result.

01:06:56.700 --> 01:07:00.700
If you just add up errors, positives and negatives may cancel out.

01:07:00.700 --> 01:07:01.700
So that's why we squared it.

01:07:01.700 --> 01:07:07.700
So now when we square it, all the errors become positive and then we take that average.

01:07:07.700 --> 01:07:10.700
So that gives us the average squared error or mean squared error.

01:07:10.700 --> 01:07:14.700
But then we want to take the square root again, because we want to interpret the number that

01:07:14.700 --> 01:07:16.700
we get as a dollar value, essentially.

01:07:16.700 --> 01:07:20.700
So now when we took squares, we converted the difference in dollars into dollars squared.

01:07:20.700 --> 01:07:25.700
So we just took, take a root again, and that just gives us a dollar value.

01:07:25.700 --> 01:07:28.700
So that's why this is called a root mean squared error.

01:07:28.700 --> 01:07:35.700
And it tells us on average, how far away we are, how far away each point is from the line.

01:07:35.700 --> 01:07:37.700
Now, this is not the only way to do it.

01:07:37.700 --> 01:07:42.700
There is also something called the root mean absolute error where you do not take squares.

01:07:42.700 --> 01:07:45.700
You actually take the absolute value, which means you simply ignore the sign.

01:07:45.700 --> 01:07:52.700
And the difference between root mean squared error and root mean absolute error is simply

01:07:52.700 --> 01:07:59.700
that outliers have a big factor or outliers contribute highly in a squared error because

01:07:59.700 --> 01:08:03.700
the, the greater the error, the greater its square is going to be.

01:08:03.700 --> 01:08:06.700
So it's going to be a bigger factor in the overall error.

01:08:06.700 --> 01:08:07.700
Okay.

01:08:07.700 --> 01:08:10.700
So that's just one thing to keep in mind, but root mean squared error.

01:08:10.700 --> 01:08:11.700
Make sure you understand this.

01:08:11.700 --> 01:08:15.700
This is the average on average, how far away we are from the line, right?

01:08:15.700 --> 01:08:18.700
And this is the formula, which is exactly the same thing.

01:08:18.700 --> 01:08:24.700
Prediction minus actual squared sum over all the predictions divided by N square root of

01:08:24.700 --> 01:08:25.700
that.

01:08:25.700 --> 01:08:27.700
And this is how you can visualize root mean squared error.

01:08:27.700 --> 01:08:28.700
So these are the rest.

01:08:28.700 --> 01:08:32.700
So suppose this is the line and then these are the actual target points for each point.

01:08:32.700 --> 01:08:36.700
You calculate how far away it is from the prediction and the prediction is simply the

01:08:36.700 --> 01:08:38.700
point on the line at the same X value.

01:08:38.700 --> 01:08:39.700
So this is a prediction.

01:08:39.700 --> 01:08:43.700
This is the actual point you calculate the residual for that point.

01:08:43.700 --> 01:08:46.700
And then you add up the squares of all the residuals, take their average and take the

01:08:46.700 --> 01:08:48.700
square root.

01:08:48.700 --> 01:08:52.700
So these are all different ways to look at the root mean squared error, but let's look

01:08:52.700 --> 01:08:55.700
at it in a way that we understand best, which is using cool.

