01:15:43.700 --> 01:15:47.700
reduce the loss and improve the fit of the line to the data.

01:15:47.700 --> 01:15:50.700
And there are a couple of strategies that are used to do this.

01:15:50.700 --> 01:15:54.700
One is called the ordinary least squares method.

01:15:54.700 --> 01:15:59.700
And the way it works is using some matrix operations and some matrix inversions.

01:15:59.700 --> 01:16:02.700
It directly arrives at the best value of W and B.

01:16:02.700 --> 01:16:05.700
So it uses a combination of calculus and linear algebra.

01:16:05.700 --> 01:16:10.700
To directly give you the values of W and B, it works well for small data sets,

01:16:10.700 --> 01:16:15.700
like data sets with a few thousand rows of data, but for larger data sets,

01:16:15.700 --> 01:16:16.700
it is very inefficient.

01:16:16.700 --> 01:16:18.700
And sometimes it takes up a lot of memory.

01:16:18.700 --> 01:16:22.700
So for that, you have this, another technique called the stochastic gradient

01:16:22.700 --> 01:16:26.700
descent technique, which is what I just talked about, which is imagine that

01:16:26.700 --> 01:16:30.700
you're on a hill and it's dark and you want to figure out how to go down.

01:16:30.700 --> 01:16:36.700
You just take steps in each direction, figure out which direction goes down and then take a few steps in that direction and then repeat, right?

01:16:36.700 --> 01:16:39.700
So that's called stochastic gradient descent.

01:16:39.700 --> 01:16:44.700
You can check out these videos for some explanation of how these work, some mathematical explanation.

