And the more important skill for you is to know how to use these rather than to understand the mathematical representation.

01:17:12.700 --> 01:17:18.700
But yeah, ordinary three squares works well for small data sets, uses some direct matrix computations.

01:17:18.700 --> 01:17:25.700
Gradient descent works well for larger data sets and uses an iterative approach.

01:17:25.700 --> 01:17:27.700
That's the actualization of how gradient descent works.

01:17:27.700 --> 01:17:31.700
It starts out with a flat line and then slowly improves both the weight.

01:17:31.700 --> 01:17:36.700
So you can see now it is improving the bias so far, but once it gets close enough, it's going to start turning the line.

01:17:36.700 --> 01:17:39.700
So it's going to start changing the line.

01:17:39.700 --> 01:17:40.700
So it computes the loss.

01:17:40.700 --> 01:17:43.700
It figures out whether it should increase or decrease the weight.

01:17:43.700 --> 01:17:48.700
It figures out whether it should increase or decrease the bias or the intercept.

01:17:48.700 --> 01:17:51.700
And that's how it keeps changing the line step by step.

01:17:51.700 --> 01:17:54.700
And then it gets to the very close to the best fit line.

01:17:54.700 --> 01:17:59.700
And it works really well for large data sets where you have millions or tens of millions of data points.

01:17:59.700 --> 01:18:02.700
So you will be applying linear regression to some really large data sets.

