And it's simply not possible to get that far out of an outlier using a line.

01:26:40.700 --> 01:26:43.700
So on average, we're actually doing much better than 4662.

01:26:43.700 --> 01:26:46.700
We are probably in the thousand, 2000 range, but yeah,

01:26:46.700 --> 01:26:51.700
this captures the fact that there are a bunch of outliers and in the model as well.

01:26:51.700 --> 01:26:57.700
So this is the whole process of machine learning that you assume a certain relationship between inputs and targets,

01:26:57.700 --> 01:27:02.700
which is the linear relationship. You then find a loss.

01:27:02.700 --> 01:27:10.700
You find a way to measure how bad your model is based on certain random parameters that you've picked initially.

01:27:10.700 --> 01:27:15.700
And then you use some kind of an optimization algorithm to improve the parameters of the model.

01:27:15.700 --> 01:27:20.700
In this case, WNB till you can minimize the loss.

01:27:20.700 --> 01:27:22.700
Okay.

01:27:22.700 --> 01:27:30.700
So we've gotten to a good point. Our model is off by 4,000 on average, which is not too bad considering that there are several outliers.

01:27:30.700 --> 01:27:32.700
Now you may be wondering, where's the WNB?

01:27:32.700 --> 01:27:37.700
Well, in scikit-learn, the W is stored in model.cof.

01:27:37.700 --> 01:27:43.700
It stands for coefficients, which are the coefficients or the weights applied to each individual feature.

01:27:43.700 --> 01:27:45.700
In this case, we're just working with age.

01:27:45.700 --> 01:27:50.700
So we have 267 is the coefficient W and then we have B here.

01:27:50.700 --> 01:27:55.700
Okay. So model dot intercept contains B and that is minus two zero nine one.

01:27:55.700 --> 01:28:01.700
So it seems like if you had picked a W of two 67 and a B of two zero nine one,

01:28:01.700 --> 01:28:04.700
we would have gotten a pretty good line and that would have been our best.

01:28:04.700 --> 01:28:07.700
That would have been the best possible model.

01:28:07.700 --> 01:28:13.700
So when you try this out later, see how close you can get without actually looking at the values of W and B.

01:28:13.700 --> 01:28:16.700
Let's maybe just plot them once again.

01:28:16.700 --> 01:28:23.700
So here we have all the points in blue and here we have our model, our line showing the predictions.

01:28:23.700 --> 01:28:27.700
So you see that our line is fitting quite nicely.

01:28:27.700 --> 01:28:35.700
I wouldn't say it's bad and it's slightly above this cluster because we also want to somehow capture the fact that there are certain outliers at the top.

01:28:35.700 --> 01:28:42.700
So if the line was somewhere here, it would technically not be giving the right information because it would not be suggesting the fact that there are outliers.

01:28:42.700 --> 01:28:46.700
So to account for all these outliers, it's slightly above the data, but that's pretty good.

01:28:46.700 --> 01:28:49.700
That's how you do linear regression using scikit-learn.

01:28:49.700 --> 01:28:57.700
You use the linear regression class, then you fit the model and then you generate predictions on new data or on existing data.

01:28:57.700 --> 01:29:04.700
Now, the linear regression class uses this optimization technique called ordinary least squares.

01:29:04.700 --> 01:29:07.700
There is another technique called gradient descent that I'd mentioned.

01:29:07.700 --> 01:29:10.700
For that, you need to use the SGD regressor.

