01:30:06.700 --> 01:30:07.700
Congratulations.

01:30:07.700 --> 01:30:20.700
You have just trained your first machine learning model and machine learning is simply the process of computing the best parameters to model the relationship between some feature and targets.

01:30:20.700 --> 01:30:30.700
So every machine learning problem that we'll solve in this entire course or in general that you'll solve anytime will generally have three components.

01:30:30.700 --> 01:30:34.700
The first thing you will have to decide is how do you want to model the relationship?

01:30:34.700 --> 01:30:37.700
Do you want to model the relationship as a linear equation?

01:30:37.700 --> 01:30:41.700
Maybe you want to model the relationship as a polynomial equation.

01:30:41.700 --> 01:30:44.700
Maybe you want to model the relationship as some kind of a decision tree.

01:30:44.700 --> 01:30:46.700
Maybe you want to model the relationship in some other way.

01:30:46.700 --> 01:30:49.700
And there are many, many, many ways to model relationships.

01:30:49.700 --> 01:31:04.700
The simplest one is to just say that the thing that we're trying to predict the target is some kind of a weighted sum of the thing that we're trying to predict the target is a weighted sum of the input features that we're interested in.

01:31:04.700 --> 01:31:16.700
For example, we could say that the charges is simply some weight w one multiplied by age, some weight w two multiplied by number of children, some weight w three multiplied by BMI plus some number B.

01:31:16.700 --> 01:31:24.700
Some intercept, right? So that's the model and every model has parameters. That's what the machine has to learn.

01:31:24.700 --> 01:31:33.700
So to understand how good the parameters are of the model, we have a cost function and there are several choices for cost function.

01:31:33.700 --> 01:31:44.700
But the idea for the cost function is that it should give you a cost, which it should give you how badly the model is performing and the lower the cost, the better the model.

01:31:44.700 --> 01:32:03.700
Okay. Once you have a cost function that can be computed given some targets and predictions, then you need to use some kind of an optimization technique to optimize the cost, which means to change the parameters of the model so that it better fits the data.

01:32:03.700 --> 01:32:14.700
And this is what this entire process looks like. You put some input data into the model and you get some outputs. You put, you take both the target and the output into the error or the loss function.

01:32:14.700 --> 01:32:21.700
You then apply an optimization method and that optimization method changes the weights and biases or changes the parameters of the model.

01:32:21.700 --> 01:32:23.700
So model gets slightly better.

01:32:23.700 --> 01:32:30.700
Then you put some more input data, you get some more output. You take that, put that into the loss function, perform optimization and improve the model.

01:32:30.700 --> 01:32:42.700
This, what we're seeing here is essentially this animation that we saw here, which is taking the line and slowly moving it so that it perfectly fits the data, right?

01:32:42.700 --> 01:32:48.700
So most of machine learning and even a lot of deep learning is basically glorified line fitting.

01:32:48.700 --> 01:32:49.700
That's all we are doing.

01:32:49.700 --> 01:32:50.700
We are fitting lines.

01:32:50.700 --> 01:33:07.700
And the fact is that the only difference is we are saying that the relationship instead of being a line is something else. And we're asking the computer to compute the parameters in that relationship or the parameters of that model.

01:33:07.700 --> 01:33:10.700
Okay. So that's, that's machine learning.

01:33:10.700 --> 01:33:17.700
And as we've seen, it just, it takes just a few lines of code to train a machine learning model using scikit-learn.

01:33:17.700 --> 01:33:24.700
You start out by creating some inputs and some targets you, for example, in the inputs in this case are non-smoker DF.

01:33:24.700 --> 01:33:28.700
And we are just picking a set of columns and currently we're just picking one column age.

01:33:28.700 --> 01:33:31.700
So the inputs need to be a two dimensional array.

01:33:31.700 --> 01:33:34.700
Then you have targets, which can be one dimensional or two dimensional.

01:33:34.700 --> 01:33:39.700
So you have a list of inputs and a list of targets and then you create a model.

01:33:39.700 --> 01:33:42.700
So here we are creating this model linear regression.

01:33:42.700 --> 01:33:47.700
And most of the time immediately, you just want to fit the model, not always, but most of the time.

01:33:47.700 --> 01:33:51.700
So if you just want to fit the model, you can create the object and immediately call dot fit on it.

01:33:51.700 --> 01:33:53.700
And that will also return the model.

01:33:53.700 --> 01:34:01.700
So you can just do this linear regression dot fit and into it, you provide the inputs and the targets and it's going to go and do its thing.

01:34:01.700 --> 01:34:02.700
It's going to do all of this for you.

01:34:02.700 --> 01:34:11.700
This class determines what kind of relationship you want to assume, like here, you want to assume the linear relationship, right?

01:34:11.700 --> 01:34:15.700
So you fit the data and that gives you a model.

01:34:15.700 --> 01:34:18.700
Then you can use that model to make predictions.

01:34:18.700 --> 01:34:23.700
So now you can just take this model and every time a new customer comes in, you ask them, Hey, what's your age?

01:34:23.700 --> 01:34:28.700
And they say 32 and you put in the number 32 and you get a certain estimate for the medical charges.

01:34:28.700 --> 01:34:33.700
That estimate can then be used to compute their insurance premium and off they go.

01:34:33.700 --> 01:34:43.700
So it automates that piece of analysis, which you had to do with maybe using several experts with years of expertise and there's a certain error.

01:34:43.700 --> 01:34:50.700
So whenever you use a model, you should be aware of what the error in your model is or what the loss, the information loss in your model is.

01:34:50.700 --> 01:34:55.700
So in this case, what we say is that, Hey, my predictions are off by maybe about three or 4,000.

01:34:55.700 --> 01:35:01.700
So you may just want to keep that in mind when you are comparing, when you are calculating the insurance premium for this person.

01:35:01.700 --> 01:35:13.700
All right. And that's your three or four step process, create inputs and targets, create a training model, create and train a model and generate some predictions with the model.

01:35:13.700 --> 01:35:20.700
And wherever possible, you also should have a measure of what is the information loss or what is the error in your model.

01:35:20.700 --> 01:35:26.700
So you create the input data, and then you put that into some kind of a model and the model gives you some predictions.

01:35:26.700 --> 01:35:37.700
Now you compare the predictions with the actual outputs and that gives you some kind of a measurement, how bad your model is your model is really bad initially.

01:35:37.700 --> 01:35:42.700
Then you run some kind of an optimization method and that improves your model.

01:35:42.700 --> 01:35:47.700
So as you give some data into the model, it learns from the data, it makes better predictions.

01:35:47.700 --> 01:35:51.700
Then you give it some more feedback using the loss function.

01:35:51.700 --> 01:36:02.700
It makes a better prediction and it gets better over time. If you can just grasp that basic idea and see how it is translated into code here, that is enough to get started.

01:36:02.700 --> 01:36:06.700
And it is a fundamentally different way of thinking about problems.

01:36:06.700 --> 01:36:09.700
If you are struggling with it a little bit, that's okay.

01:36:09.700 --> 01:36:16.700
Once you struggle through it, and once you get the idea, once it clicks, what's happening here, you will suddenly realize the potential of what it makes possible.

01:36:16.700 --> 01:36:27.700
Because now instead of let's say having several years of expertise in analyzing or evaluating insurance applications, you just have this one system.

01:36:27.700 --> 01:36:37.700
You just feed in some input and it's going to give you an output and you know how much it is going to be off by and you can use it.

01:36:37.700 --> 01:36:40.700
What about some of the other features?

01:36:40.700 --> 01:36:47.700
So far we've used just the age feature to estimate charges adding another feature like BMI is very straightforward.

01:36:47.700 --> 01:36:53.700
So we simply assume this relationship now instead of saying charges is some weight W plus B.

