02:13:53.700 --> 02:13:59.700
So just a quick summary of how you approach a machine learning problem.

02:13:59.700 --> 02:14:04.700
First step is to explore the data and find different correlations between inputs and targets.

02:14:04.700 --> 02:14:08.700
Understand what leads to a certain target.

02:14:08.700 --> 02:14:13.700
Maybe even go back and do some research, whether there is a cause effect relationship or is there just a correlation?

02:14:13.700 --> 02:14:19.700
And is it possible that the correlation may just go away or is it possible that the correlation is unfair in any way?

02:14:19.700 --> 02:14:20.700
For example, sure.

02:14:20.700 --> 02:14:24.700
Maybe you should not be using race as a factor in computing insurance, right?

02:14:24.700 --> 02:14:28.700
So that's something you should be conscious of as a data scientist.

02:14:28.700 --> 02:14:30.700
Then you pick the right model.

02:14:30.700 --> 02:14:31.700
In this case, we pick linear regression.

02:14:31.700 --> 02:14:33.700
You pick the light loss functions.

02:14:33.700 --> 02:14:37.700
In this case, we picked root mean squared error and you pick the right optimizer.

02:14:37.700 --> 02:14:45.700
In this case, we pick least squares and all of these three are captured in the linear regression class from scikit-learn.

02:14:45.700 --> 02:14:47.700
So for different problems, you have to pick different models.

02:14:47.700 --> 02:14:49.700
Sometimes you have to pick decision trees.

02:14:49.700 --> 02:14:51.700
Sometimes you have to pick neural networks or something else.

02:14:51.700 --> 02:14:59.700
Then you scale the numeric variables and one hot, you scale the numeric variables so that they all have a mean of zero standard deviation of one.

02:14:59.700 --> 02:15:03.700
You perform one hot encoding for categorical data.

02:15:03.700 --> 02:15:09.700
And this is not something we covered today, but this is something that we'll cover next time about setting aside a test set.

02:15:09.700 --> 02:15:10.700
Then you train a model.

02:15:10.700 --> 02:15:13.700
Then you make predictions and compute the loss.

02:15:13.700 --> 02:15:20.700
Now we didn't do this today, but one thing you should do is you should actually set aside 10% of your data,

02:15:20.700 --> 02:15:24.700
a small fraction of your data, just for testing and reporting the results of your model,

02:15:24.700 --> 02:15:29.700
because the model like the ones we have created today are designed to be used in the real world,

02:15:29.700 --> 02:15:31.700
not only on the data that we train them on.

02:15:31.700 --> 02:15:37.700
So what people typically do is when they get some data from let's say a product manager or somebody,

02:15:37.700 --> 02:15:44.700
whoever has given us the insurance data, we take 10% of the data and then we set it aside forever.

02:15:44.700 --> 02:15:46.700
Then we do all our modeling and everything.

02:15:46.700 --> 02:15:50.700
Let's say we build a linear regression model on 90% of the data.

02:15:50.700 --> 02:15:58.700
And then when we report the loss, when we actually report the metrics that, Hey, I built this model and this is the result it gives.

02:15:58.700 --> 02:16:00.700
And this is the kind of loss that I've been able to achieve.

02:16:00.700 --> 02:16:03.700
Or this is the kind of error that my model has.

02:16:03.700 --> 02:16:09.700
You should be then reporting that error on the test data, which means on the 10% of the data,

02:16:09.700 --> 02:16:12.700
which you set aside at the beginning and did not use for training.

02:16:12.700 --> 02:16:16.700
And you can do this in scikit-learn using this train test split.

02:16:16.700 --> 02:16:18.700
So what that does is it takes some inputs.

02:16:18.700 --> 02:16:19.700
It takes some targets.

02:16:19.700 --> 02:16:26.700
It takes a 0.1 like a fraction and it gives you a bunch of 90% of the data, which you can use for training,

02:16:26.700 --> 02:16:30.700
which is input strain and target strain, and then 10% of the data, which you can use for testing.

02:16:30.700 --> 02:16:31.700
Okay.

02:16:31.700 --> 02:16:34.700
And we'll talk about this in a lot more detail the next time.

02:16:34.700 --> 02:16:47.700
But what you will notice is that the score on your test set or the loss on your test set is going to be always higher than the loss on your training set because your model has not seen these examples.

02:16:47.700 --> 02:16:51.700
So model is bad is worse at predicting examples that it has not seen.

02:16:51.700 --> 02:16:52.700
And this is again, true.

02:16:52.700 --> 02:16:55.700
A lot of machine learning models, when you put them out into the real world,

02:16:55.700 --> 02:17:01.700
they perform worse, even though you may get a really, you get like a 98% accuracy when you're training the model.

02:17:01.700 --> 02:17:04.700
But when you put it out, it's less than 30%.

02:17:04.700 --> 02:17:08.700
So we will talk about a lot of techniques in this course on how to counter that.

02:17:08.700 --> 02:17:12.700
This is called overfitting and we'll talk in a lot of detail about that.

02:17:12.700 --> 02:17:21.700
So the right strategy is to set aside a test set before you train a model and then train the model and then make predictions and compute the loss and report the results on your test set.

02:17:21.700 --> 02:17:28.700
And we will apply this process over and over to several problems with different kinds of models over the next few weeks.

