We pick a random weight W2 and random weight B and we fix them.

02:32:40.700 --> 02:32:42.700
So W1 W2 B we fix them.

02:32:42.700 --> 02:32:46.700
Then we calculate this quantity for a given input.

02:32:46.700 --> 02:32:50.700
Let's say we have some information about the city Melbourne.

02:32:50.700 --> 02:32:52.700
We take the temperature at Melbourne.

02:32:52.700 --> 02:32:54.700
We take the rainfall at Melbourne.

02:32:54.700 --> 02:32:57.700
We multiply them with W1 and W2 and we add B.

02:32:57.700 --> 02:33:02.700
So we get this quantity X1 W1 plus X2 W2 plus B.

02:33:02.700 --> 02:33:06.700
Then we have an additional step here.

02:33:06.700 --> 02:33:12.700
So in linear regression, we were done at this point, but in logistic regression, we have an additional step.

02:33:12.700 --> 02:33:20.700
We take this quantity and then we put it through this activation function called Sigma or called sigmoid.

02:33:20.700 --> 02:33:25.700
It's represented by this character Sigma, but it stands for sigmoid.

02:33:25.700 --> 02:33:34.700
What the sigmoid function does is it takes this output of this linear combination and it squishes it between the range zero to one.

02:33:34.700 --> 02:33:39.700
Okay. So the exact details are not very important about how this function does.

02:33:39.700 --> 02:33:49.700
So, but what it does is it takes this linear combination or this quantity that we get X1 W1 plus X2 W2 plus B and it squishes it into the range zero to one.

02:33:49.700 --> 02:34:03.700
And then that value, which we get out of the Sigma function is inferred as the probability of the class that we're trying to predict.

02:34:03.700 --> 02:34:09.700
So since we're trying to do a yes, no prediction, which is it will rain tomorrow or it will not rain tomorrow.

02:34:09.700 --> 02:34:20.700
So the output of Sigma Z output of Sigma will be a number between zero and one, and that number will indicate what our model thinks is the probability that it will rain tomorrow.

02:34:20.700 --> 02:34:31.700
Okay. And then we compare those probabilities with the actual values because we have historical data, we can see whether our model is predicting the right probabilities or not.

02:34:31.700 --> 02:34:36.700
If it is not predicting the right probabilities, then we penalize it by giving it a high loss.

02:34:36.700 --> 02:34:42.700
If it is predicting the right probabilities, then we penalize it, then we penalize it less by giving it a low loss.

02:34:42.700 --> 02:34:49.700
But overall, we come up with a loss for the model and for logistic regression, the loss is called the cross entropy loss.

02:34:49.700 --> 02:34:55.700
It has a formula like this. Again, the details are not that important.

02:34:55.700 --> 02:35:00.700
The important thing is that there is a small change in the structure of the model, which is this function Sigma.

02:35:00.700 --> 02:35:10.700
And then this loss is a value, which is high if the model predicts bad probabilities and the loss is low if the model predicts good probabilities.

