03:01:13.700 --> 03:01:18.700
So moving right ahead, before we train the model, we have to learn a few best practices.

03:01:18.700 --> 03:01:25.700
So we are going to learn a few best practices that now you will start to see coming up over and over again with every model we build.

03:01:25.700 --> 03:01:31.700
Now, while building real world machine learning models, it is quite common to split a dataset into three parts.

03:01:31.700 --> 03:01:43.700
The first part is the training set and the training set is used to train the model, which means that we take the inputs from the training set, put them into the model, which is randomly initialized with some random weights.

03:01:43.700 --> 03:01:56.700
We then take the outputs or the predictions of that model, compare them with the targets, the actual targets and computer loss using a loss function. And then we put them through the optimizer to change the models weights using an optimization technique.

03:01:56.700 --> 03:01:59.700
So that's machine learning. That's a machine learning training workflow.

03:01:59.700 --> 03:02:06.700
The training set is used to train the model, but remember that your model is not going to be used on the training data.

03:02:06.700 --> 03:02:11.700
The training data in this case, for example, is historical data, but we want to use the model on new data.

03:02:11.700 --> 03:02:19.700
So it will not be accurate to report the model's performance on just the training set.

03:02:19.700 --> 03:02:22.700
And that is why we create two other sets.

03:02:22.700 --> 03:02:25.700
We create a validation set and a test set.

03:02:25.700 --> 03:02:32.700
Now a validation set is used to evaluate the model during training because training is often not a one step process.

03:02:32.700 --> 03:02:36.700
Often you train a little bit and then see if you've got a good enough result, maybe train some more.

03:02:36.700 --> 03:02:47.700
Maybe you change some parameters in the model, maybe instead of using a logistic regression model, you use a polynomial logistic regression model, or maybe you change the optimization algorithm.

03:02:47.700 --> 03:02:52.700
Maybe you change from least squares to gradient descent to bilinear solver, et cetera, et cetera.

03:02:52.700 --> 03:03:02.700
So while you're training the model, you will want to experiment with a lot of different tweaks to the model structure itself and to the optimization process itself, or maybe even to the loss function.

03:03:02.700 --> 03:03:03.700
You can even change the loss function.

03:03:03.700 --> 03:03:15.700
So as you want to tune all of these different ways of training the model, you should be using what is called a validation set, which is simply a fraction of the dataset.

03:03:15.700 --> 03:03:30.700
And you shouldn't use it to train the model, but you should use it to evaluate the model, which means that once you have trained the model using the training set, you then make some predictions on the validation set and simply check what the score on the validation set is.

03:03:30.700 --> 03:03:36.700
So instead of now using that score to once again, train the model or improve the weights, you don't do that.

03:03:36.700 --> 03:03:46.700
You use just the training set for training and you use the validation set simply for checking how well the model is doing while you are still experimenting with the model.

03:03:46.700 --> 03:03:59.700
And picking a good validation set is an essential, a very essential, probably the most, one of the most important parts of a machine learning project, because you want your model to generalize well.

03:03:59.700 --> 03:04:00.700
And you can learn more about it here.

03:04:00.700 --> 03:04:03.700
I've linked to a blog about picking good validation sets.

03:04:03.700 --> 03:04:05.700
And then finally, there is a test set.

03:04:05.700 --> 03:04:14.700
So then there's a third set and this test set is used to compare different models or compare different approaches by different people.

03:04:14.700 --> 03:04:21.700
For example, you have certain data sets, which are public data sets that are used by researchers around the world.

03:04:21.700 --> 03:04:33.700
Now with these data sets, if everybody reports their accuracies or their performance on a different validation set, maybe somebody picks a first 10% of data.

03:04:33.700 --> 03:04:35.700
Somebody picks a last 10% of data.

03:04:35.700 --> 03:04:38.700
Somebody picks a middle 10% or a random 10%.

03:04:38.700 --> 03:04:41.700
Then those results will not be comparable.

03:04:41.700 --> 03:04:47.700
So that's why typically it's a common practice to standardize a test set for a data set.

03:04:47.700 --> 03:04:52.700
And you will often see that you will get a training data and then you will get a test data.

03:04:52.700 --> 03:05:00.700
So when you have training data, you split it into training and validation, but the test data is what is standardized and you're not supposed to use it for training.

03:05:00.700 --> 03:05:02.700
You're not even supposed to use it for validation.

03:05:02.700 --> 03:05:06.700
You should only use it at the end to report the model's final accuracy.

03:05:06.700 --> 03:05:10.700
And for many data sets, test sets are provided separately.

03:05:10.700 --> 03:05:18.500
And then in a lot of cases, for example, in Kaggle data science competitions, you are not given the actual targets for the test set.

03:05:18.500 --> 03:05:30.700
So you are simply given the inputs for the test set, and you have to then make some predictions for the test set, create a CSV file with your predictions, submit it to Kaggle and then Kaggle will compare them with the actual data for the test set.

03:05:30.700 --> 03:05:33.700
And then give you the result or give you the score.

03:05:33.700 --> 03:05:35.700
So this is called a hidden test set.

03:05:35.700 --> 03:05:38.700
And a lot of companies tend to do this as well as a data scientist.

03:05:38.700 --> 03:05:44.700
So once you're given a project, you're the, you will not be given the entire data.

03:05:44.700 --> 03:05:51.700
So you'll be given maybe 80% of the data with labels, and then you will be given another 20% of the data with the labels hidden from you.

03:05:51.700 --> 03:06:02.700
So you will then, once you train all your models, you will take that 20% of data and you will make predictions, give it back to whoever has given you the project and they will tell you how well your model is doing in the real world.

03:06:02.700 --> 03:06:03.700
Okay.

03:06:03.700 --> 03:06:12.700
So this is a very important piece of machine learning and you will often see when you start out with machine learning that the models that you train perform really well in training.

03:06:12.700 --> 03:06:20.700
You get to like 98, 99, 100% accuracy, but when you put them out into the real world, they get like 30, 40% accuracy.

03:06:20.700 --> 03:06:26.700
And this phenomenon is called overfitting where your model is optimizing too much for the training set.

03:06:26.700 --> 03:06:34.700
So to give you an analogy to think about the training validation and test set, think about how school works or how high school works.

03:06:34.700 --> 03:06:41.700
So now in India, we have 12 grades and in grade 12, what happens is you have a textbook and you have classes.

03:06:41.700 --> 03:06:47.700
So you read the textbook and in the textbook, for example, for a math scores in the textbook, you have some questions.

03:06:47.700 --> 03:06:54.700
So now you solve the questions in a textbook and you use that to improve your understanding of mathematics.

03:06:54.700 --> 03:07:01.700
And one way to do it is to actually learn the concepts, but the other way to do is to simply memorize the answers, right?

03:07:01.700 --> 03:07:09.700
And your model, because you are simply trying to optimize for the lowest possible laws, your model over time will start memorizing training examples.

03:07:09.700 --> 03:07:16.700
Just like in a lot of school systems, people start memorizing answers to questions.

03:07:16.700 --> 03:07:24.700
So to avoid blind memorization, what people tend to do is schools conduct exams.

03:07:24.700 --> 03:07:27.700
So school can schools conduct a midterm school, schools conduct quizzes.

03:07:27.700 --> 03:07:31.700
So they conduct exams at various places during the school year.

03:07:31.700 --> 03:07:33.700
And you can think of that as the validation set.

03:07:33.700 --> 03:07:39.700
So that is where the model or you face questions that you have not already solved.

03:07:39.700 --> 03:07:43.700
And obviously your performance is a little bit worse, but more importantly,

03:07:43.700 --> 03:07:49.700
you need to learn the actual concepts or the underlying relationships rather than simply memorizing answers, right?

03:07:49.700 --> 03:07:55.700
So that's a validation set, but then you have these board exams or what you might call the SATs,

03:07:55.700 --> 03:08:02.700
which are used to compare the performances of students across the nation or across the world, even so you can think of that as the test set.

03:08:02.700 --> 03:08:11.700
So that is like at the end, at the very end of your school year, you have to give this one exam and everybody in the country will be ranked based on that exam.

03:08:11.700 --> 03:08:15.700
So that's the training set validation set and test set training set is what you use to learn.

03:08:15.700 --> 03:08:21.700
Validation set is what you use to improve yourself and change your approach while you're still building a model.

03:08:21.700 --> 03:08:24.700
And the test set is to report your final performance.

03:08:24.700 --> 03:08:25.700
Okay.

03:08:25.700 --> 03:08:31.700
And as a general rule of thumb, this is not, this is not set in stone, but as a general rule of thumb,

03:08:31.700 --> 03:08:34.700
you can use about 60% of the data for training.

03:08:34.700 --> 03:08:38.700
You can use about 20% data for validation and 20% for test.

03:08:38.700 --> 03:08:44.700
And this is typically good enough if you're working with the, let's say tens of thousands of rows or hundreds of thousands.

03:08:44.700 --> 03:08:51.700
If you have more data, millions of rows, then you can shrink the size of your validation and test sets and use more for training.

03:08:51.700 --> 03:09:00.700
If you have less data, then you may have to even keep a larger fraction, but it really depends on problem to problem.

03:09:00.700 --> 03:09:07.700
And if a separate test set is already provided to you, let's say you're given a training set and a test set separately,

03:09:07.700 --> 03:09:16.700
then you can do a training validation split, which could be like a 75 25 or a 70 30 split.

03:09:16.700 --> 03:09:22.700
So typically this pick or this division of the validation and test set is done randomly,

03:09:22.700 --> 03:09:25.700
especially when the dataset does not have any inherent order.

03:09:25.700 --> 03:09:34.700
So if you have just random datasets or random rows in the data, it is very common practice to just pick a random subset of rows for creating the test set and the validation set.

03:09:34.700 --> 03:09:39.700
And this can be done using the train test split utility from scikit-learn.

03:09:39.700 --> 03:09:47.700
So we import from scikit-learn.model selection, train test split, and you give train test split the data that you want to split.

03:09:47.700 --> 03:09:51.700
So in this case, we want to split the raw DF, the entire dataset that we have.

03:09:51.700 --> 03:09:56.700
And we want to extract out first 20% and set that aside as the test DF.

03:09:56.700 --> 03:10:00.700
So as the test data frame, so I'm just setting test size equals 20%.

03:10:00.700 --> 03:10:04.700
Now, which 20% do we want to extract?

03:10:04.700 --> 03:10:07.700
So we can specify a random number and we don't have to.

03:10:07.700 --> 03:10:12.700
If we don't, then it will pick something automatically, but we can specify a random number.

03:10:12.700 --> 03:10:14.700
So in this case, I'm just specifying the number 42.

03:10:14.700 --> 03:10:21.700
And what this will do is this will initialize a random number generator and then using that random number generator, certain rows will be picked.

03:10:21.700 --> 03:10:29.700
So a certain fraction of 20% fraction of the data will be put into test DF and the remaining will be put into what I'm calling train while DF.

03:10:29.700 --> 03:10:30.700
Okay.

03:10:30.700 --> 03:10:41.700
Now the benefit of random state is that it gives you the guarantee that every time you pass 42 as the random state, the same fraction, the same 20% fraction will get picked as a test set.

03:10:41.700 --> 03:10:47.700
So the benefit here is that each time you rerun the notebook, your test and validation sets will not change.

03:10:47.700 --> 03:11:00.700
So we want a random fraction, which is picked randomly once, but we also want it to be fixed across our runs because otherwise we will not be able to compare with each run of our notebook, whether we're doing better or not.

03:11:00.700 --> 03:11:01.700
Right.

03:11:01.700 --> 03:11:03.700
So you want to pick a random fraction, but we want to fix it.

03:11:03.700 --> 03:11:05.700
And that's what random status for.

03:11:05.700 --> 03:11:06.700
Okay.

03:11:06.700 --> 03:11:09.700
So now we've created a test data frame, which is 20% of data.

