Validation data frame contains 28,000 test contains 28,000.

03:12:03.700 --> 03:12:04.700
Okay.

03:12:04.700 --> 03:12:06.700
So this is what you will do for most datasets.

03:12:06.700 --> 03:12:11.700
You will pick a random sample as a validation set and a random sample as a test set.

03:12:11.700 --> 03:12:14.700
If you're given a test set already, this will go away.

03:12:14.700 --> 03:12:16.700
Maybe you'll have a separate CSV file for tests.

03:12:16.700 --> 03:12:20.700
So you'll simply do a 75 25 split between training and validation.

03:12:20.700 --> 03:12:21.700
Okay.

03:12:21.700 --> 03:12:29.700
Now, one other thing I want to add here is when you're working with dates, which we are in this case, fortunately or unfortunately.

03:12:29.700 --> 03:12:33.700
So when you're working with dates, there is an additional consideration to keep in mind,

03:12:33.700 --> 03:12:39.700
because we want to take this model, which has been trained on historical data.

03:12:39.700 --> 03:12:42.700
And then we want to use it in the future.

03:12:42.700 --> 03:12:43.700
Okay.

03:12:43.700 --> 03:12:51.700
So that means that your model ideally when it is making prediction on a certain date,

03:12:51.700 --> 03:12:56.700
it should not have seen any data that came after that date, right?

03:12:56.700 --> 03:13:01.700
Because you should not train your model to learn from future data to predict past data.

03:13:01.700 --> 03:13:05.700
So whenever you're working with, this is called time series data, or whenever you're working with dates,

03:13:05.700 --> 03:13:11.700
it is often a better idea to separate training, validation, and test sets with time.

03:13:11.700 --> 03:13:15.700
So what you want to do is maybe if we just check out the years in the data.

03:13:15.700 --> 03:13:18.700
So we have, this is what the distribution of the data looks like.

03:13:18.700 --> 03:13:25.700
We have these many, we have a bunch of values for 2007, a bunch of them for 2008, nine, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14.

03:13:25.700 --> 03:13:30.700
And we are presumably going to use the model to make predictions in 2018 or 2019.

03:13:30.700 --> 03:13:34.700
So here is a good way to divide the training and validation and test sets.

03:13:34.700 --> 03:13:39.700
How about we take the data till 2014 as the training set.

03:13:39.700 --> 03:13:43.700
So we train the model using data till 2014.

03:13:43.700 --> 03:13:47.700
Then we use the data from 2015 as the validation set.

03:13:47.700 --> 03:13:52.700
So now the model will be evaluated on data from the future data.

03:13:52.700 --> 03:13:54.700
It has not already seen in the past, right?

03:13:54.700 --> 03:14:02.700
So we use 2015 as the validation set, and then we use 2016 and 2017 as the test set.

03:14:02.700 --> 03:14:11.700
So our final accuracy of the model will be reported by its performance on 2016 and 2017.

03:14:11.700 --> 03:14:16.700
While the model has been trained and evaluated on data till 2014 and 2015.

03:14:16.700 --> 03:14:21.700
The benefit of this is that because we are going to use a model in 2018 and 2019.

03:14:21.700 --> 03:14:27.700
So the test sets score is going to very well reflect how the model will perform in real life.

03:14:27.700 --> 03:14:32.700
On the other hand, let's say if you pick 2011 as the test set, then it's possible that the model

03:14:32.700 --> 03:14:38.700
may use some information from 2010, 2012 to become a better predictable, better predictor

03:14:38.700 --> 03:14:42.700
for 2011 than it would be for 2018, right?

03:14:42.700 --> 03:14:45.700
Because it has access to data from the past as well as the future.

03:14:45.700 --> 03:14:50.700
So what I want to want you to take away from this is whenever you're working with dates

03:14:50.700 --> 03:14:56.700
or with time series data, it's often a good idea to take the last fraction as the test set and maybe

03:14:56.700 --> 03:14:59.700
a fraction just before that as the validation set.

03:14:59.700 --> 03:15:00.700
Okay.

