03:17:11.700 --> 03:17:19.700
So next best practice or next step in a machine learning problem is to identify the input and target columns.

03:17:19.700 --> 03:17:22.700
And often this is something that people tend to mess up as well.

03:17:22.700 --> 03:17:26.700
Just in the hurry to get to the modeling process.

03:17:26.700 --> 03:17:29.700
So not all columns in a dataset are useful for training a model.

03:17:29.700 --> 03:17:41.700
In fact, in the current dataset, if we look at raw DF, you can see here that we have a date column and the date column is different for each day.

03:17:41.700 --> 03:17:44.700
In fact, it only contains dates from 2008 to 2017.

03:17:44.700 --> 03:17:49.700
Now, presumably we're going to use the model in 2020 or 2021 and onwards.

03:17:49.700 --> 03:17:54.700
So the date will never be something like this, right?

03:17:54.700 --> 03:18:00.700
So the model will, so there's no point training the model on these dates or dates, which it has seen in the past.

03:18:03.700 --> 03:18:05.700
If it is going to be used in the future, right?

03:18:05.700 --> 03:18:07.700
So date is not a useful input to the model.

03:18:09.700 --> 03:18:14.700
On the other hand, minimum temperature definitely is because the model is going to see temperatures in the same ranges.

03:18:14.700 --> 03:18:18.700
It may see, it may not see 13.4, but it may see 13.5.

03:18:18.700 --> 03:18:19.700
It may see 13.1.

03:18:19.700 --> 03:18:23.700
So this is a useful thing to have maximum temperature is again, a useful thing to have.

03:18:23.700 --> 03:18:31.700
So all of these rows, apart from date, like location, main temp, max temp, et cetera, these are definitely useful for a prediction.

03:18:31.700 --> 03:18:33.700
So we definitely want to use these.

03:18:33.700 --> 03:18:35.700
Similarly, sometimes you will have ID columns.

03:18:35.700 --> 03:18:42.700
For example, in the last example of insurance or medical charges prediction, there was certain IDs.

03:18:42.700 --> 03:18:52.700
Sometimes you will have names of people like you, you may have names of a customer or maybe you may have emails of customers or you may have like a customer ID.

03:18:52.700 --> 03:18:56.700
Or some unique identifier, which is different for every row.

03:18:56.700 --> 03:19:02.700
So whenever you have these ID columns, you should completely ignore those because those are just unique.

03:19:02.700 --> 03:19:04.700
And that is not useful information to use.

03:19:05.700 --> 03:19:09.700
Then there is obviously also a target column, the column that we want to predict.

03:19:09.700 --> 03:19:14.700
We want to take all of this information and we want to use it to predict the rain tomorrow column.

03:19:15.700 --> 03:19:20.700
So we want to make sure that we don't use the rain tomorrow column as an input for sure.

03:19:20.700 --> 03:19:21.700
Right?

03:19:21.700 --> 03:19:25.700
You don't want to use a weather train tomorrow to predict whether it rain tomorrow.

03:19:25.700 --> 03:19:27.700
You don't the rain tomorrow is a target.

03:19:27.700 --> 03:19:40.700
And that is why, and it seems like an obvious thing, but in a lot of cases, you will see that you are training a model and then you train the model and you suddenly see a hundred percent accuracy, a hundred percent training accuracy, a hundred percent validation, a hundred percent test accuracy.

03:19:41.700 --> 03:19:49.700
And you debug and debug, or maybe you just think you've won, but ultimately you will figure out that you were actually using the target as an input.

03:19:49.700 --> 03:19:51.700
I do this all the time whenever I'm training models.

03:19:51.700 --> 03:19:59.700
So that's why one thing you should try and do is very clearly identify what are the inputs to your model and what are the outputs and what are the things you need to ignore.

03:20:00.700 --> 03:20:02.700
So I'm going to create two lists here.

