03:54:24.260 --> 03:54:28.100
up one feature does not mess up the loss.

03:54:28.100 --> 03:54:33.540
Then we take that weighted combination and we apply the sigmoid function, which ultimately

03:54:33.540 --> 03:54:36.820
gives us a number between zero to one for each input.

03:54:36.820 --> 03:54:42.460
So for each set of inputs for each specific row of data, we get a number as zero between

03:54:42.460 --> 03:54:44.600
zero and one as the output of the model.

03:54:44.600 --> 03:54:48.080
The number represents a probability of the input being classified as yes.

03:54:48.080 --> 03:54:50.780
So that's how we interpret the number from the model.

03:54:50.780 --> 03:54:56.660
Then we use the cross entropy loss to evaluate how good the probability is with respect to

03:54:56.660 --> 03:54:57.660
the results.

03:54:57.660 --> 03:55:04.500
Now, obviously intuitively, what we want is if the target is yes or one, then we want

03:55:04.500 --> 03:55:07.000
the probability to be high, very close to one.

03:55:07.000 --> 03:55:11.900
If the target is no or zero, then we want the probability to be no.

03:55:11.900 --> 03:55:13.340
And that's what this formula captures.

03:55:13.340 --> 03:55:14.580
This is called cross entropy.

03:55:14.580 --> 03:55:20.660
We won't get into it, but the idea is it penalizes bad predictions.

