04:12:12.900 --> 04:12:13.900
Yes.

04:12:13.900 --> 04:12:14.900
We call that a true.

04:12:14.900 --> 04:12:18.440
And then, and the model predicted, no, so if the actual value was no, and the model

04:12:18.440 --> 04:12:22.420
predicted, no, we call that a true negative, which means it was able to identify a negative

04:12:22.420 --> 04:12:25.860
correctly that it will not rain correctly.

04:12:25.860 --> 04:12:31.900
And similarly, if the actual value is yes, and your model predicted, yes, we call that

04:12:31.900 --> 04:12:36.420
a true positive, which means that it is going to rain tomorrow and your model did, it did

04:12:36.420 --> 04:12:38.780
predict that it is going to rain tomorrow.

04:12:38.780 --> 04:12:41.180
So we call that a true positive.

04:12:41.180 --> 04:12:43.660
So these are both cases in which your model has succeeded.

04:12:43.660 --> 04:12:46.020
These are both the success scenarios.

04:12:46.020 --> 04:12:50.260
Then there are two types of scenarios, error scenarios, and they are called the type one

04:12:50.260 --> 04:12:54.060
error and type two error, or false positive and false negative.

04:12:54.060 --> 04:12:55.380
Once we have false positive.

04:12:55.380 --> 04:13:01.280
So false positive is when it is not going to rain tomorrow or the actual value is no,

04:13:01.280 --> 04:13:05.980
but your model has predicted yes.

04:13:05.980 --> 04:13:08.940
So model has predicted that yes, it is going to rain tomorrow.

04:13:08.940 --> 04:13:14.340
And then similarly here, we have a false negative when the, where the actual value is, yes,

04:13:14.340 --> 04:13:17.660
it is going to rain tomorrow, but your model has predicted no.

04:13:17.660 --> 04:13:22.940
Now, depending on the business case or what you want to use the model for, you may want

04:13:22.940 --> 04:13:26.420
to very closely look at false positives or false negatives.

04:13:26.420 --> 04:13:32.740
For example, if you want to use this model to predict whether a baseball game should

04:13:32.740 --> 04:13:38.580
be hosted tomorrow in the open, then false negatives are a big problem.

04:13:38.580 --> 04:13:42.960
You may want to avoid false negatives where it is going to rain tomorrow, but your model

04:13:42.960 --> 04:13:44.900
predicts that it is not going to rain.

04:13:44.900 --> 04:13:48.300
So if you have a high number of false negatives, that means you are in for a surprise where

04:13:48.300 --> 04:13:53.460
you get 50,000 people and people are playing baseball and starts to rain.

04:13:53.460 --> 04:13:55.500
That's why maybe you want to optimize.

04:13:55.500 --> 04:14:00.580
You want to reduce the false negatives and you're not just interested in the accuracy.

04:14:00.580 --> 04:14:05.320
On the other hand, let's say it's a different kind of model, which is predicting whether

04:14:05.320 --> 04:14:07.440
you may have best breast cancer.

04:14:07.440 --> 04:14:13.260
Then at that point, you may want to take a close look at false positives before let's

04:14:13.260 --> 04:14:17.060
say recommending, I don't know, chemotherapy to somebody.

04:14:17.060 --> 04:14:21.420
You want to make sure that your model, you want to make sure that it is not a false positive.

04:14:21.420 --> 04:14:24.240
And for that while training the model, you want to make sure that the number of false

04:14:24.240 --> 04:14:25.880
positives is low.

04:14:25.880 --> 04:14:30.060
So in general, depending on the kind of problem you may want to optimize either for false

04:14:30.060 --> 04:14:32.900
positives or for false negatives.

