05:23:48.960 --> 05:23:54.720
If it is between 50 to $80,000, then maybe you check if the office is close to your home.

05:23:55.440 --> 05:23:57.680
If it is not, then you decline the offer.

05:23:57.680 --> 05:24:06.880
Otherwise you check if the office, if the company provides a cab facility, and if it, if it doesn't, then maybe you decline the offer.

05:24:06.880 --> 05:24:08.000
Otherwise you accept the offer.

05:24:08.640 --> 05:24:14.480
So this kind of a strategy is how we make a lot of decisions in the real world.

05:24:14.480 --> 05:24:16.720
In fact, this is how a lot of processes are set up.

05:24:16.720 --> 05:24:27.680
And if you think carefully, this is how programs are also set up or where we write a lot of if-else, if-else statements to come to a certain decision.

05:24:27.680 --> 05:24:39.680
Now a decision tree in machine learning works exactly the same way, except that we let the computer figure out the optimal structure and hierarchy of decisions instead of coming up with the criteria manually.

